use /lib
from ./chat-input import ChatInputScope
from ./message-list import MessageList
from /ui/components/history-list import HistoryListScope
from /ui/components/scroll-column import ScrollColumnScope

export scope PromptEditor:
    concept: {PromptEditorConcept}

concept PromptEditorConcept:
    pitch:
        An interface for sending tasks to a local coding harness like Claude
        Code, written directly with compiled-on-depand Piton.

    shape: {PromptEditorShape}

shape PromptEditorShape:
    description:
        Not a chat. Each prompt sent is a task for the harness, tasks can
        queue, and each task shows its output until it finishes. There are no
        message bubbles.

    basicDesignAndLayout:
        The @{MessageList} fills all available space except for a
        @{ChatInputScope} along the bottom. From top to bottom it holds the
        previous tasks row, the header for the latest task, the latest task's
        output, and the queue.

        Before any task has been sent, the space shows a hint instead, which
        wraps when the view is narrow. It reads
        "Write a prompt below. Enter adds a line; Ctrl/Cmd+Enter sends it."
        or, while no project is open, "Open a project to start prompting."

    chatHistory: @{MessageList}

    previousTasks:
        Previous tasks are a @{HistoryListScope} along the top of the view,
        listing every task sent. Its row counts every task but the latest,
        which heads the view: "No previous tasks", "1 previous task", or
        "N previous tasks". Expanded, the list takes the place of the latest
        task and its output, and the row reads "Back to the latest task".

    header:
        The latest task heads the view whether or not the harness is working:
        its status, with a spinner while it is under way, the name of the
        hidden anchor it was compiled from, and the compiled markdown the
        harness actually received, which scrolls once it gets tall. Until it
        has compiled, the prompt as typed is shown instead.

    queue:
        overview:
            Prompts can still be sent while the harness is working: they wait
            in a queue, in the order they were sent, and are sent one at a
            time. While the harness is working, the chat input's send button
            reads "Queue".

            A question from the Ask tab never queues. It runs straight away,
            even while the harness is working on a task.

        placement:
            The queue sits along the bottom of the view, below the output and
            directly above the chat input, with a border along its top. It is
            not part of the header. A row saying how many prompts are queued,
            with a chevron, expands to list each queued prompt in order, a line
            or two of each, with a button to cancel it. Each prompt in the list
            is numbered, with its number, text, and cancel button vertically
            centred in its row, and the list scrolls once it gets tall, with a
            @{ScrollColumnScope}. The
            same area holds the auto-send switch and the "Send next" button.
            The queue is shown only while it has anything in it.

        cancel:
            A queued prompt that has not been sent yet can be cancelled, which
            removes it from the queue for good. A prompt already sent is no
            longer in the queue.

        autoSend:
            A "Send next automatically" switch picks between sending the next
            queued prompt as soon as the harness is free, and waiting. It starts
            switched on. Switching it on while the harness is free sends the
            next queued prompt straight away. Whenever the harness is free and
            the queue is not empty, a "Send next" button sends the first queued
            prompt. A prompt that fails to compile or run does not stop the
            queue.

        persistence:
            Queued prompts are saved in the project's `.suspense/queue`
            directory, each as its hidden anchor's source, imports included,
            the way prompt history is, and named so they list in the order they
            were queued. The queue is restored whenever the project is opened;
            a restored queue waits for "Send next" rather than sending on its
            own. A queued prompt's file is removed when it is cancelled, or
            when it is sent, at which point it is saved to the prompt history
            like any other prompt.

    buildingTheSpec:
        Before a Code, Chain, or Spec prompt is sent, `piton build` runs in the
        project, so the compiled spec the harness reads is up to date. It runs
        when the prompt is actually sent, so a queued prompt builds when its
        turn comes, not when it is queued. The task shows "Building" until the
        build is over. A build that fails does not stop the prompt, since the
        prompt may be the one to fix the spec: the task's output starts with
        an error saying the build failed, so the compiled spec may be out of
        date, with what the build reported. A question from the Ask tab is sent
        without building.

    history:
        Every task sent is saved in the project's `.suspense/history` directory
        as its hidden anchor's source, named by the second it was sent and the
        anchor's name. Once its run is over, what came of it is saved beside it
        in a `.json` file of the same name: the compiled userPrompt the harness
        received, each line the harness printed, and any error. Opening the
        project brings back every task in order, with its output replayed from
        that record. A task with no record shows as "Not recorded".

        Every question asked from the Ask tab is logged the same way, but in
        the project's `.suspense/asks` directory instead, so it never comes back
        as a task: its hidden anchor's source, and beside it a `.json` record
        of what came of it. The record is saved when the run is over, and also
        when the question is closed or replaced while it runs, with as much as
        the harness had done. A question that fails before it reaches the
        harness is logged too, with the error in its record.

    chatInput: @{ChatInputScope}
